99
00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:38.970
Well, you know, Kendra, we love your podcast, The Lazy Genius.
肯德拉，我们很喜欢你的播客《懒人天才》。

100
00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:43.770
And we would love for you to tell our audience, what is a lazy genius?
我们很想请你告诉我们的听众，什么是“懒人天才”？

101
00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:51.430
A lazy genius is someone who is a genius about the things that matter and lazy about the things
“懒系天才”是指在重要的事情上精益求精，而在无关紧要的事情上

102
00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:52.210
that don't.
选择偷懒的人。

103
00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:58.910
So the thing that the problem is that there are a lot of voices that are telling us all
问题在于，有很多声音在告诉我们

104
00:04:58.910 --> 00:05:00.170
the different things that should matter.
各种各样所谓“重要”的事情。

105
00:05:00.830 --> 00:05:04.730
And we want to be like good rule followers and like decent people.
我们想做循规蹈矩的好人，做个像样的人。

106
00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:06.150
And we're like, OK, well, this should matter.
于是我们想，好吧，这事儿应该很重要。

107
00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:07.330
I'll work hard on this.
我要在这上面努力。

108
00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:08.050
I'll do this thing.
我要把这事儿做好。

109
00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:11.090
And then we just like end up with adrenal fatigue.
结果最后我们只会精疲力竭。

110
00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:12.490
It's like a whole thing.
就是这么回事。

111
00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:20.870
So I want to give people permission to choose what matters to them based on their own lives
所以我希望能让大家有底气去选择对自己真正重要的事，基于各自的生活、

112
00:05:20.870 --> 00:05:24.370
and their own seasons of life and their personalities and their families and all of that.
人生阶段、性格、家庭等等因素。

113
00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:28.630
And then put energy into things that matter to them.
然后把精力投入到那些对他们重要的事情上。

114
00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:33.210
Like it's not that you have to like messy hair don't care your entire life away.
这并不是说你要一辈子都过着“头发乱糟糟，我才不在乎”的生活。

115
00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:34.690
You can care about stuff.
你当然可以有所追求。

116
00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:36.030
You can be good at stuff.
你当然可以把事情做好。

117
00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:38.150
Like I think that's a wonderful thing.
我觉得那是一件很棒的事情。

118
00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.530
You want to be a genius at certain things in your life.
你当然希望在生活中某些方面成为天才。

119
00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:44.770
But in order to do that, you also got to let some other stuff go.
但为了做到这一点，你也必须学会放下一些别的东西。

120
00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:46.390
You got to be lazy about some stuff.
你得学会在某些事情上偷懒。

121
00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:53.010
And I just give people principles to help do that in their own way.
我只是提供一些原则，帮助人们用自己的方式做到这一点。

122
00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:58.490
And then also I have this podcast that kind of breaks it down topically to kind of help you be a
此外，我还有一个播客，按主题进行拆解，帮助你

123
00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.070
lazy genius about whatever the thing is.
在任何事情上都能成为“懒系天才”。

124
00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:04.630
And so I've been doing that for – I've been teaching people how to be a lazy genius since 2016.
我从2016年就开始教大家如何成为“懒系天才”了。

125
00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:10.570
Well, Kendra, the very first podcast I heard of yours was called something like
肯德拉，我听的你的第一个播客标题大概是

126
00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:13.590
How to Organize Paper in Your Home.
《如何整理家中的纸质文件》。

127
00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.410
Oh, she talked about that so much, Kendra.
噢，肯德拉，她当时讲了好多关于那个的内容。

128
00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:17.230
All the paper that comes in your home.
家里收到的所有纸质文件。

129
00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:18.930
And this was my introduction to you.
那就是我认识你的开始。

130
00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:21.670
I didn't really know what a lazy genius was yet.
当时我还不知道什么是“懒系天才”。

131
00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:30.690
And so when I clicked on that podcast, I thought you were going to literally prescribe to me what to do with all the papers that come into my house,
所以当我点开那个播客时，我以为你会直接告诉我该怎么处理家里收到的所有纸张，

132
00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:37.450
all of my kids' artwork, all of the school forms, all of the, you know, insurance follow-up from when you go to the doctor,
所有的孩子画作、学校表格，还有你看完医生后收到的那些保险单据，

133
00:06:37.450 --> 00:06:41.710
all the things that accumulate in a pile or multiple piles.
所有堆成一堆或好几堆的东西。

134
00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:46.790
And to my surprise, you did not tell me what to do with the paper.
令我惊讶的是，你并没有告诉我该怎么处理这些纸。

135
00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:52.090
You asked me what mattered to me about these piles of paper.
你问我，这些成堆的纸里，什么对我来说才是重要的。

136
00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:57.290
And it was such this light bulb for me because you're right.
那一刻我茅塞顿开，因为你说得对。

137
00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:04.490
I watch Instagram and there's all these videos of like special frames that I can put my kids' artwork in.
我刷Instagram时，总能看到各种视频，推荐我买那种可以放孩子画作的特殊画框。

138
00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:05.970
And I'm like, should I be doing that?
我心想，我是不是也该这么做？

139
00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:10.750
Should I be buying the frames where I cycle the different paintings in and out?
我是不是该买那种可以轮换展示画作的画框？

140
00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:11.530
I tried it.
我试过。

141
00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.170
I never cycled a single thing.
但我从来没换过哪怕一张画。

142
00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:19.850
And what I learned from your podcast, Kendra, was that a lot of that paper didn't matter to me.
肯德拉，从你的播客里我学到的是，很多纸质文件对我来说根本不重要。

143
00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:21.930
And I could let it go.
我可以选择放下它们。

144
00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:29.630
It was okay if we looked at a piece of the artwork and we appreciated it and then we let it go.
如果我们欣赏过孩子的画作，然后把它处理掉，这完全没问题。

145
00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:31.910
And it was great.
那感觉棒极了。

146
00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:36.070
It was very freeing because you helped me name what did matter to me about it.
这让我感到非常解脱，因为你帮我明确了什么对我来说才是重要的。

147
00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:41.350
And then you helped me allow myself to be lazy about the other parts.
然后你又帮我学会了在其他方面允许自己偷懒。

148
00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:43.050
And it was transformative.
这简直是脱胎换骨的改变。

149
00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:44.170
It really was.
